"# Appendix Y — Tick extras

`tick/*` verbs turn a phase (0..1) into a scalar or picked value.
The three you already know (`tick/sine`, `tick/pulse`, `tick/ease`)
are the classics. Four more filled in 2026-07-22 fill common gaps.

## `tick/triangle` — linear peak instead of sinusoidal

Where `tick/sine` swings smoothly, `tick/triangle` ramps linearly:

    (tick/triangle 0.0)   ; => -1
    (tick/triangle 0.25)  ; =>  0
    (tick/triangle 0.5)   ; =>  1
    (tick/triangle 0.75)  ; =>  0

Optional amplitude scales the peak: `(tick/triangle 0.5 2)` is 2 not
1. Kids reach for triangle waves when they want a mechanical
back-and-forth (bouncing paddle, sweeping searchlight) that looks
more \"rigid\" than sine.

## `tick/mod-n` — bucket the phase into n slices

Given a phase 0..1 and an integer n, `tick/mod-n` returns an integer
0..n-1. Perfect for indexing a step sequencer:

    (define patterns (list 'kick 'snare 'hat 'snare))
    (list-ref patterns (tick/mod-n (tick/phase 0.7 1) 4))
    ; => 'snare

## `tick/pattern` — pick from a list by phase

The sibling of `mod-n`, `tick/pattern` takes the list directly and
returns the chosen item. Same math, one less step:

    (tick/pattern 0.5 '(a b c d))   ; => c

## `tick/lissajous` — parametric xy curve

Feed a phase and two frequency ratios; get a point on the Lissajous
curve. Two circles rotating at different rates trace a signature
figure — kids love the shapes.

    (tick/lissajous 0.25 3 2)
    ; => (-1 ~0)

Optional delta shifts the y channel's phase for the Y offset that
lets you sweep through the full pattern gallery.

Every verb above runs through `bin/motoi eval` today.
")
